  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThis morning...Temperatures are generally higher than at this time\nyesterday with some inland valley locations as much as 10 to 15\ndegrees higher. Several locations broke or tied the maximum\ntemperature records for Oct 2nd. See our Record Event Report.\nSkies are clear but overnight low temperatures will likely remain\nabove normal west of the mtns due to the gusty offshore winds and\nhumidities in the 30-40 percent range.\n\nHigh pressure aloft and moderate to locally strong offshore flow \nwill cause the heat to peak this weekend, with daytime high\ntemperatures 15-25 degrees above normal for the inland valleys and\ncoastal areas. Daytime high temperatures will reach the upper 90s\nto low triple-digits within a mile or two of the beaches in spite\nof there being a weak sea breeze. The high pressure aloft will\nkeep the marine layer very shallow and the offshore flow will \nkeep any low clouds or fog from moving onshore.\n\nThe offshore flow weakens on Monday and we could see some minor \nrelief at the immediate coast by Tuesday. The lower temperatures\nwill slowly spread inland through the middle of next week but the\nhigh pressure aloft will persist. As a result, daytime high \ntemperatures will remain in the upper 90s to low triple-digits for\nthe inland valleys through next Thursday. A return of marine layer\nlow clouds/fog to the coastal areas seems unlikely before Wed\nnight/Thu morning.\n\nBeyond next Thursday...Forecast details remain quite uncertain,\nhowever a slim majority of ensemble members across model platforms\nshows the high pressure ridge weakening in response to a trough of\nlow pressure approaching from the northwest. The forecast reflects\nthis with a trend toward noticeably lower temperatures beginning\nnext Friday, although high temperatures on Friday will still be as\nmuch a 15 degrees above normal in the western portions of the\ninland valleys.",
